 Propose only bounded edits anchored to the actual block. For insert_after, original_text is an existing insertion anchor. An issue may have no proposal when the available material cannot support a responsible patch. A title/summary is not complete evidentiary proof. Every proposal goes to an independent verifier using available full evidence; do not claim your own proposal is verified. No search, citations outside material_index, whole-article rewrite, or forced edit for an unsupported review concern. Any known_fixed_issues constrain this controlled experiment but must still be judged against the actual snapshot.
 
+When known_fixed_issues is supplied, it is the authoritative issue set for this controlled run: do not rediscover, add, drop, or reorder issues. Return the issues array with exactly the supplied issue_id values in their supplied order, retaining the supplied issue metadata rather than inventing alternative metadata. Proposals must use those issue IDs and their supplied target anchors and operations; an unsupported issue may have no proposal or a no_change proposal. When known_fixed_issues is absent, use the normal discovery behavior above.
+
 The preserve list contains exact literal substrings from original_text that must remain byte-for-byte unchanged. Use [] when semantic preservation is required but no literal span must be fixed; the independent verifier always checks full semantic preservation.
 
 A historical source handle alone is not proof of paper identity or claim support. Compare the material title and actual content with stable paper IDs, DOI and title in selected_source_identities when provided. Treat conflicting title, DOI, stable identity or content as an unresolved identity/evidence gap: do not invent a mapping, silently replace handles globally, or accept an edit that depends on that conflict. Authors should return no_change with the conflict reason; verifiers should return insufficient_evidence. Reviewers should identify the specific gap without guessing a repaired identity.
